carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot18) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Document upload name collisions retain their recoverable JSON response but
    never attach the path-bearing exception to a log event. The packaged UI
    validation also waits for tickler deletion to commit before asserting it.
  * Enforce the hermetic Struts DTD validation in CI and clean up the snapshot
    changelog metadata used by Debian package linting.
  * Exclude transient eChart note locks from the optional demonstration dataset
    and reject them at package-build time.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:56:11 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot17) resolute; urgency=medium

  * A tainted error body that cannot be replaced is now discarded instead of
    turning into a second, manufactured 500. When something commits the
    response behind the sanitisation filter, the two post-chain replacement
    sites had no guard -- unlike their mid-chain siblings, which sit under a
    catch -- so the "already committed" failure escaped the outermost filter
    as a container 500. The captured body is dropped instead: nothing unsafe
    reaches the client, and the client keeps the response it was already sent.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 21:15:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot16) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The response-sanitisation filter's pass-through stream no longer flushes
    when something inside the chain closes it. Flushing commits the response
    without assigning a Content-Length, which turned forwarded pages and
    streamed downloads chunked and, more importantly, permanently disabled the
    filter's own late-error branch for them -- the branch that still replaces
    a stack trace that escaped capture. Closing is now a no-op; the container
    sets Content-Length and closes the buffer when the request ends.
  * Upgrades warn when the Tomcat context descriptor, a conffile, was kept
    from a local edit and so lacks suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward="false".
    Without that attribute forwarded pages come back empty and forwarded error
    pages come back as 500s, with nothing logged.
  * Upload-name collisions no longer log the exception. Its message is the
    destination path, which ends in the uploader's filename, and scanned
    clinical documents are routinely named after the patient.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot15) resolute; urgency=medium

  * JSP-to-JSP forwards deliver their body again, and an error page that sets
    a 4xx and then forwards answers that 4xx with its body instead of a raw
    500. Tomcat 11 defaults suspendWrappedResponseAfterForward to true, which
    finishes the response when a forward returns and strands the forwarded
    page's output in javamelody's writer buffer — silently: 200 with an empty
    body, or a manufactured 500, and nothing logged for either. The context
    descriptor now pins the attribute to false.
  * ResponseSanitizationFilter no longer converts an already-rendered page
    into a 500 when something inside the chain commits the response before
    its replay: it appends the safe captured body and logs the premature
    commit at ERROR. Its capture now also honours resetBuffer()/reset() (a
    forward clears uncommitted output; stale captured prefixes went with it),
    and its passthrough streams shield the real response from a mid-chain
    close.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot14) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Upload rejections are reported instead of vanishing. The "input" result
    added in ~snapshot5 forwarded the eDocs add-document form to the documents
    page without an error, so a rejected upload rendered a clean document list
    and looked like a page refresh; it now carries the error, and three more
    upload pages that built a message and then hid or skipped it (the eForm
    upload and import partials, and the BC lab upload) report it too.
  * Upload rejection messages say what actually happened. They were hardcoded
    to "Empty files not accepted." for every multipart rejection, including a
    file refused for its size; they now carry the multipart layer's own wording.
  * Empty-file uploads no longer 500 on mcedt/autoUpload, mcedt/upload,
    lab/newLabUpload or lab/CMLlabUpload, which the ~snapshot5 sweep missed.
    Four mappings it added could never fire and are removed.
  * The deleted-eForms list no longer throws on every draw. Giving the table a
    thead made DataTables initialise, which reached a drawCallback calling a
    function that does not exist anywhere in the codebase.
  * Restore on the deleted-eForms list works. It built its POST after page load
    so CSRFGuard never injected a token and every attempt was rejected with 403.
  * Drug search failures no longer delete the DrugRef status panel. The error
    handler wrote into the container holding the database name, version and
    date, and only a page reload brought them back.
  * The admin shell no longer paints "Sorry but there was an error: 0 error"
    over controls whose href is javascript:void(0), matching the eForm fix.
  * A document stored successfully is no longer reported as failed when a
    later step (audit log, inbox routing, queue link) fails, which sent users
    to re-upload and put a second copy of the scan in the chart.
  * The standalone eForm editor and the deleted-eForms list no longer throw
    "ReferenceError: $ is not defined". Neither page loads jQuery; both worked
    only because the Administration shell that injects them provides one.
  * The DrugRef rebuild (updateDrugrefDB, method=updateDB) rejects GET. It is a
    mutation and CSRFGuard does not validate tokens on GET, so a link or an
    <img src> could trigger a full database rebuild.
  * The legacy uploader treats XHR status 0 as a failure. Status 0 means no HTTP
    response was produced at all, so an aborted or dropped upload was being
    reported to the user as "Upload complete".
  * A rejected eDocs upload no longer answers a raw 500. The rejection page set
    a 4xx status and wrote a body, and the response-sanitisation filter cannot
    replay that combination — it fails to reset the buffer and the request comes
    back as 500. The browser page now renders at 200 and the XHR branch uses
    sendError, which the filter does not disturb.
  * A rejected eDocs upload renders the rejection page instead of a blank page.
    The previous forward rebuilt the documents page from request parameters
    that no longer exist, because the multipart parse that failed is what put
    the request on this path in the first place.
  * Restoring an eForm no longer answers 405 after succeeding. The success
    result was a forward, which keeps the POST method, and the deleted-list gate
    permits POST only for the editor — the same defect delete was fixed for.
  * Add Link and Add HTML no longer answer 500 when a required field is blank.
    Each validation branch put the empty string under the request attribute the
    documents JSP casts to a form bean, so a missing description, type or URL
    produced a ClassCastException instead of the field message.
  * Restoring an eForm rejects GET. The action performed the restore on any
    request method, so an <img src=".../eform/restoreEForm?fid=N"> on a page an
    eForm administrator loaded restored that form with no CSRF token — giving
    the page its token protected the intended path but not the bypass.
  * Authorization failures answer 403 instead of 200. securityError.jsp set no
    status, so the legacy uploader (which treats anything under 400 as done)
    reported "Upload complete" for an upload the privilege check had refused.
  * A rejected replacement file on the eDocs EDIT form no longer dumps the whole
    documents page into the 350x500 edit popup with the ADD panel open — which
    invited the clinician to upload again and put a second copy in the chart.
  * Upload rejections are classified by the multipart layer's own marker rather
    than by searching its message for the word "empty" — that message embeds the
    submitted filename, so a file named "empty-form.pdf" rejected for its size
    was reported as zero-length.
  * Eight more upload pages report multipart rejections instead of re-rendering
    their form with no explanation.
  * The eForm footer no longer throws on the five eForm pages that load no
    jQuery of their own, which previously pre-empted each page's own scripts.
  * WAF: the attack-rfi exclusions are withdrawn. Those CRS rules are anchored
    at the start of the argument and cannot match an eForm document, so the
    exclusion only widened the rule surface. attack-lfi, which does fire, stays.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 03:30:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot5) resolute; urgency=medium

  * eForm HTML editor saves work from the Administration panel. The panel's
    shared registerFormSubmit() helper posted the save with $(form).serialize(),
    which both re-encoded the multipart form as application/x-www-form-urlencoded
    AND silently dropped any file input; it now posts multipart forms as
    FormData, so the save arrives multipart like the standalone editor (and file
    uploads through the panel, e.g. eForm import, now carry their file).
  * eForm authoring exclusions (1050, 1060, 1070) unhook attack-lfi and
    attack-rfi on their own argument. Rule 930110 is CRITICAL at paranoia
    level 1 and matches a bare "../" in ARGS, which any eForm carrying a
    parent-relative image or stylesheet path contains by construction.
  * eForm administration no longer reports "Sorry but there was an error:
    0 error" after a delete that in fact succeeded. The delete and restore
    controls carry href="javascript:void(0);" alongside the contentLink class,
    and the shared footer's delegated handler AJAX-loaded that literal href;
    the request cannot succeed, so jQuery reported status 0 and the false
    banner replaced the page content while the real delete completed.
  * The deleted-eForms list initialises without "DataTables warning: table
    id=tblDeletedEforms - Incorrect column count". The table shipped its header
    and body rows with no thead/tbody, so DataTables registered zero columns
    against six-cell rows and aborted its init.

 -- CARLOS EMR Maintainers <michael@maplecreekmedical.ca>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 21:30:00 +0000

carlos-emr (2026.09.0~snapshot4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Default rename: the database is now `carlos` (was the inherited `oscar`)
    and the document store is now CarlosDocument (was OscarDocument), with a
    `carlos` instance segment: /var/lib/carlos-emr/CarlosDocument/carlos.
  * One-time migrations, applied only when upgrading from before this version
    and only where the OLD STOCK DEFAULT is still in effect — an explicitly
    configured CARLOS_DB_NAME or a post-upgrade OscarDocument directory is
    never touched:
    - the `oscar` schema is renamed to `carlos` in place via the new
      idempotent `carlos-ctl db-rename-schema` (atomic multi-pair RENAME
      TABLE; flyway_schema_history and its checksums move unchanged), then
      CARLOS_DB_NAME / CARLOS_BACKUP_DB_NAME are updated. If MariaDB is down
      the rename defers to the next configure (dpkg-reconfigure carlos-emr).
    - the document store is moved to CarlosDocument with an OscarDocument
      compat symlink left behind, so absolute paths already stored in the
      database (faxes, email attachments) keep resolving.
  * The weekly restore drill accepts both directory spellings: the newest
    snapshot right after the upgrade still carries pre-rename paths.
  * Upgrades no longer leave the EMR down behind a 502. carlos-emr,
    carlos-emr-drugref and carlos-emr-eform-renderer each restart
    carlos-emr.service, so one apt transaction restarts it three times; against
    the unit's StartLimitBurst=6/30min an install followed by an upgrade inside
    the same half hour exhausted the budget and systemd refused the last restart
    with "start-limit-hit". Package-driven restarts — and `carlos-ctl
    start`/`restart` — now clear the start counter first, and the two dependent
    postinsts recover a unit an earlier one in the same transaction left failed.
    Crash-loop protection is unchanged: Restart=on-failure with RestartSec=15s
    still trips the same limit in about 90 seconds.
  * WAF: saving a drawn provider signature works through the front door.
    Provider preferences posts the pad's canvas.toDataURL() output as
    ARGS:signatureData to /provider/providerSignatureStamp, and the
    `data:image/...;base64` prefix scored CRS 941130/941170 over the anomaly
    threshold, 403-ing the save with the page's own "Save failed" banner — the
    same data-URI false positive already excluded for the consultation signature
    pad and eForm image saves, on the one signature route they missed. New
    per-argument exclusion 1080 unhooks only that argument, only on POST to that
    route; the action still requires write access and strictly validates and
    size-caps the base64 image server-side.

